description:
Show a file to the USER by opening it in an editor window. Input: the FULL path of the file. Output: only whether the window opened.

The path must be complete, from the drive or the root folder down to the file name: C:\Users\name\Documents\report.docx on Windows, /Users/name/Documents/report.docx on macOS, /home/name/Documents/report.docx on Linux. A bare file name or a relative path is refused - there is no folder it could be looked for in - and so is a path where no file exists; either way nothing opens and the error says why. A tool that creates or saves a file reports the full path it wrote to: pass that path exactly as reported, never shortened to the file name.

This function does NOT read the file: none of its content comes back to you, so calling it teaches you nothing about what is inside. Use it only when the user asked to SEE a file.

To find out what is inside a file, do not call this one:
- a text file (.md, .txt, .csv, .json, .log, ...) -> read it with run_script (cat on macOS/Linux, Get-Content on Windows);
- a document (.docx, .xlsx, .pptx, .pdf, .odt, .ods, .odp, .rtf, .epub, .xps, .djvu, ...) -> open it with the document tools, which return its text, cells or form fields. They read far more formats than the four obvious ones, so hand the file over instead of assuming it is unsupported.

Return value:
{
"role": "tool", // Required constant field
"status": "success" | "error",
"error_message": "string | null" // Error details if status = 'error'
}

Example function call parameter: { "path": "/home/user/document.txt" }

Example successful response:
{
"role": "tool",
"status": "success",
"error_message": null
}

Example error response:
{
"role": "tool",
"status": "error",
"error_message": "File not found: /home/user/document.txt. Nothing was opened. Check the full path and call file_opener again with the path of a file that exists."
}
